{
  "case_id": "2021-20-659",
  "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nAMIR H. ALI, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nJONATHAN Y. ELLIS, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nJOHN D. MOORE, ESQUIRE, New York, New York; on behalf of the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amir H. Ali",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Second Circuit holds that a criminal proceeding terminates in the accused's favor only if it affirmatively indicated that the accused is innocent.\nThat is wrong. A criminal proceeding terminates in the accused's favor when it ends and the prosecution has failed to obtain a conviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Ali, before we get to the termination issue, favorable termination issue, don't we have to address whether or not there actually can be a malicious prosecution case or claim based upon a Fourth Amendment seizure?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Just one more question, counsel. You do not embrace the Laskar test, right? You don't --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. ALI",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ALI: So, Your Honor, the Laskar test was that there's no requirement of an indication of innocent, and what you were looking for was whether there was a judgment that is inconsistent with innocence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, what if they're dismissed pursuant to an agreement that says, okay, you were -- you were the number two person in this vicious gang and you've killed five people and all that, but we want you to testify against the number one person, and in exchange, we're going to dismiss the charges? Is -- is -- is that consistent with innocence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. ALI",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ALI: Under our test, the dismissal in that case would be a favorable termination, but, as common law courts recognized, the manner of dismissal, and so that agreement, would all but doom the Fourth Amendment claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: As I understand what happened, your client was arrested without probable cause, and, eventually -- he was held for 39 hours and then released on his own recognizance, and sometime during that period the criminal complaint was filed. Would he have been released any sooner had the criminal complaint not been filed?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. ALI",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ALI: Your Honor, what we'll have to prove -- and at least one of the seizure theories -- we, of course, have the Second Circuit precedent that compelled attendance and the -- the conditions are a seizure."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Am I to understand you correctly that what you're claiming is a Manuel-type fabrication of evidence to initiate the charges?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: How are we supposed to decide what the elements of a malicious prosecution claim are under the Fourth Amendment if we're not sure such a thing exists?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We believe Respondent forfeited -- with respect to those seizures, he -- he forfeited any challenge to those seizures."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And -- and just to finish up, are -- are -- on that theory, are people also seized even when they're given a citation but free to go, released on bail, who receive a civil process for a -- a subpoena to appear at trial? Are those persons seized?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, I think the reason the bounds of that rule hasn't been litigated in this case and I can't answer your question is that Respondent never raised it below."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Ali, the tort of unreasonable seizure pursuant to legal process, do you accept that that requires the plaintiff to prove the elements or some of the elements of malicious prosecution, including absence of probable cause?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "At common law, the favorable termination element served three purposes, namely, avoiding collateral tax on criminal proceedings through civil suits, avoiding parallel proceedings over guilt and probable cause, and avoiding inconsistent criminal and civil judgments."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What exactly is that constitutional right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: A quick question. Do you -- do you or the government have any idea of how many, approximate, malicious prosecution claims against states or the subdivisions are brought in the United States every year?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JOHN D. MOORE ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS\nMR. MOORE: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nThe Second Circuit correctly interpreted the favorable termination requirement of Petitioner's malicious prosecution claim. The circuit's rule requires that a petitioner -- that a plaintiff bringing a malicious prosecution claim demonstrate that the underlying criminal charges ended in a manner indicative of innocence, meaning that the charges terminated in favor of the criminal defendant in a way that reflected on the merits of those claims -- those charges rather."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0003-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that the qualified immunity and probable cause are the things that stop frivolous claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, whether I answer the upstream question or the downstream question, I have to be interpreting the Fourth Amendment here, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. ALI",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-90d2b7f7a5a2-0004-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 99,
        "start": 99
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Second Circuit decided this case on the basis that the favorable termination rule we have applied to certain Section 1983 claims requires indications of innocence. It does not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-20-659",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "90d2b7f7a5a2f73a04dda83f9629557d7f9d668e53f4766db47e396b7cf83df7",
      "evidence_id": "unavailable-90d2b7f7a5a2-103",
